00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:46.580
The plan for today is to start
by recapping the big idea from

00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:48.740
Thursday's class, which we sort
of rushed through at the very

00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:53.340
end when trying to recap it
then, which is what makes a

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:55.920
matrix invertible.
I want us to deeply understand

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:59.760
that coming out of today.
We will look at a few special

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:03.140
cases.
We will think about how you

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:06.520
might find the inverse of a
matrix using a formula for

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:09.920
smaller matrices, some
considerations about solving for

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:11.280
the inverse in practice,

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:15.580
and this related idea of the
determinant of a matrix.

00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:19.380
And then we will also start to
talk about the next big idea,

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:24.160
which is how to project not onto
one vector, but onto the column

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:27.260
space of a matrix.
or, in other words, onto the

00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:31.020
span of several vectors.
And that is, in other words,

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:33.120
multiple linear regression.

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:36.900
So this is now the point where
we connect to the ideas that we

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:38.860
saw at the start of the
semester.

00:01:38.860 --> 00:01:40.760
But first, we need to deeply
understand

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:42.480
what makes a matrix invertible.

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:45.380
So suppose A is an invertible
matrix.

00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:49.880
What does that mean? Like, what
does that tell you about A?

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:52.500
Can someone tell me?

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:54.040
There are several things this
tells you about A.

00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:57.100
What's the first thing this
tells you about A?

00:01:57.100 --> 00:02:01.600
Yeah, A is a square matrix.

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:07.900
Remember, invertibility is only
a property of square matrices.

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:10.620
So if I tell you A is
invertible,

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:12.540
well, then it has to be square.

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:16.160
But it tells you more than that.
It's not just any square matrix.

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:19.780
What is also true about A if it
is invertible?

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:24.280
Yeah? I have a full rank.

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:29.700
A is full rank.
And what does that mean?

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:33.460
I have a rank of A.

00:02:33.460 --> 00:02:34.440
Good.

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:40.440
The rank of A is as large as
physically possible.

00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:44.560
What are some other ways to
interpret that?

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:45.520
Columns are literally
independent.

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:46.520
Yeah,

00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:48.760
AKA,

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:53.520
A's columns are linearly
independent.

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:01.910
Give me another way of
interpreting that.

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:06.740
So yes, it's true that if A is
invertible, then its rank has to

00:03:06.740 --> 00:03:10.180
be equal to n, which is its
number of rows and columns.

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:13.140
All of that implies that its
columns are linearly

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:15.320
independent, but it also implies
a few other things.

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:15.820
Yeah.

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:17.140
The rows are linearly
independent? Yeah.

00:03:17.140 --> 00:03:19.390
It also means,

00:03:20.660 --> 00:03:22.910
let's see, also,

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:35.840
what

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:39.590
else does this tell you?

00:03:41.260 --> 00:03:42.260
Yeah.

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:47.020
Is the null space only the zero
vector?

00:03:47.020 --> 00:03:48.020
Good.

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:50.840
It tells you that the null space
of A

00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:54.590
is the trivial null space.
It's just the zero vector.

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:57.960
This is really just another way
of saying A's columns are

00:03:57.960 --> 00:03:59.280
linearly independent,

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:02.340
because this is saying the only
way you can make the zero vector

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:04.880
as a linear combination of A's
columns

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:07.960
is by taking zero of each
column.

00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:09.300
OK, awesome.

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:14.940
There's another way of thinking
about this, which we will get to

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:15.980
in just a little bit.

00:04:15.980 --> 00:04:18.260
I'll write it for now just so
it's here and we will come back

00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:19.140
to it.

00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:23.640
The determinant of A is not
equal to 0.

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:27.160
What is the determinant of A?
We're gonna get there in just a

00:04:27.160 --> 00:04:28.160
second.

00:04:28.840 --> 00:04:29.160
Now,

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:30.280
okay, these are all great.

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:33.240
It's good that we have this
understanding of what it means

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:36.220
for a matrix to be invertible in
terms of its properties

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:38.640
involving its rows and columns.

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:41.740
But what does the inverse
satisfy?

00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:46.420
Suppose A inverse exists.

00:04:49.820 --> 00:04:55.020
What properties does it satisfy?

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:02.340
We just discussed what it means
for A to have an inverse.

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:03.460
Now,

00:05:03.460 --> 00:05:04.660
that inverse itself,

00:05:04.660 --> 00:05:05.940
what does it do?

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:10.440
What is its relationship to A?

00:05:12.140 --> 00:05:16.640
What is the relationship of A
and its inverse?

00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:22.100
Yeah? If you multiply it by A,
you get the identity.

00:05:22.100 --> 00:05:23.100
Good.

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:28.000
Not only is that true,

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:32.500
it is the unique matrix where
that happens.

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:48.360
It's the unique matrix where A
times its inverse is the

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:51.860
identity.
And it's also true that A

00:05:51.860 --> 00:05:54.980
inverse times A is the identity.

00:05:54.980 --> 00:05:56.160
In general,

00:05:56.160 --> 00:05:58.760
we know that AB is not the same
as BA.

00:05:58.760 --> 00:06:01.500
But for the inverse of a square
matrix,

00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:03.200
this commutativity is
guaranteed.

00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:07.700
It doesn't matter what order you
multiply A by its inverse by,

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:11.520
what you get back is the
identity.

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:12.220
Now,

00:06:12.220 --> 00:06:12.900
good.

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:16.020
We've mostly spent our time on
Thursday and today so far are

00:06:16.020 --> 00:06:19.820
thinking about how do we know if
a matrix is invertible? And on

00:06:19.820 --> 00:06:22.980
Thursday, we spent time thinking
about this in the context of a

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:24.320
type of function.

00:06:24.320 --> 00:06:26.680
Can you remind me what the name
of that special type of function

00:06:26.680 --> 00:06:27.680
was?

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:34.140
It's made up of two words.

00:06:34.140 --> 00:06:39.320
What did we talk about all of
Thursday?

00:06:39.320 --> 00:06:41.570
Yeah, linear transformations.

00:06:41.660 --> 00:06:42.660
Remember,

00:06:48.460 --> 00:06:50.800
The function T of x

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:56.560
is equal to matrix A

00:06:56.560 --> 00:06:58.060
times vector x.

00:06:58.060 --> 00:07:01.820
You can think of this as an
operation happening to the

00:07:01.820 --> 00:07:03.320
vector x.

00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:07.620
The way I talked about this is
you imagine the vector x goes

00:07:07.620 --> 00:07:09.870
through this machine,

00:07:10.140 --> 00:07:13.260
which multiplies it by A.

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:17.220
And what comes out is a new
vector,

00:07:17.220 --> 00:07:18.040
Ax.

00:07:18.040 --> 00:07:21.140
And we spent some time looking
at some special cases of

00:07:21.140 --> 00:07:26.620
matrices where the action that A
does to x is describable in

00:07:26.620 --> 00:07:27.300
English.

00:07:27.300 --> 00:07:30.400
Things like it rotates x or it
stretches its components

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:31.400
separately.

00:07:32.540 --> 00:07:34.060
Does that kind of sound
familiar?

00:07:34.060 --> 00:07:35.480
OK, good.

00:07:35.480 --> 00:07:36.520
And so there,

00:07:36.520 --> 00:07:41.280
the idea was that a matrix is
invertible if its associated

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:44.420
linear transformation, which
again is just the function that

00:07:44.420 --> 00:07:47.120
takes that matrix and multiplies
it by incoming vectors,

00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:50.870
if that operation is invertible.

00:07:51.780 --> 00:07:53.840
That's something to review.

00:07:53.840 --> 00:07:56.660
Now that was our focus so far,

00:07:56.660 --> 00:07:59.840
this more theoretical idea of
what makes a matrix invertible.

00:07:59.840 --> 00:08:00.940
Now I want to ask,

00:08:00.940 --> 00:08:04.100
how would you actually find the
inverse?

00:08:04.100 --> 00:08:04.820
OK.
OK,

00:08:04.820 --> 00:08:05.820
now,

00:08:18.600 --> 00:08:21.740
let me give you an example of a
matrix.

00:08:21.740 --> 00:08:23.780
Why don't we use 1,

00:08:23.780 --> 00:08:24.340
5,

00:08:24.340 --> 00:08:25.840
2, 12.

00:08:26.360 --> 00:08:29.460
Now, if you've read the notes,
or you've maybe seen this

00:08:29.460 --> 00:08:30.460
before,

00:08:30.600 --> 00:08:34.520
you may be familiar with the
formula that tells you the

00:08:34.520 --> 00:08:37.000
inverse of a two by two matrix.
And I'll tell it to you in just

00:08:37.000 --> 00:08:38.760
a little bit.
Not expecting you to already

00:08:38.760 --> 00:08:39.560
know it.

00:08:39.560 --> 00:08:40.900
What I'm getting at, though,

00:08:40.900 --> 00:08:45.120
is you don't strictly need to
know any formula ahead of time

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:46.380
to find the inverse.

00:08:46.380 --> 00:08:48.860
There is like a first principles
way

00:08:48.860 --> 00:08:53.360
of finding the inverse of any
matrix.

00:08:55.180 --> 00:08:59.680
What shape must the inverse of
this matrix have?

00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:03.180
I'm trying to find the inverse
of this matrix, but I don't know

00:09:03.180 --> 00:09:04.720
any formula that does it.

00:09:04.720 --> 00:09:07.960
So I'm trying to figure it out
from scratch.

00:09:07.960 --> 00:09:13.160
How would I go about doing this?

00:09:13.160 --> 00:09:14.160
Yeah?

00:09:15.020 --> 00:09:17.600
It needs to be a square.
It needs to be a square 2 by 2

00:09:17.600 --> 00:09:18.600
matrix.

00:09:18.740 --> 00:09:22.980
So why don't I say the inverse X
is the matrix

00:09:22.980 --> 00:09:28.920
X, Y,

00:09:29.160 --> 00:09:30.080
Z,

00:09:30.080 --> 00:09:31.080
W.

00:09:36.080 --> 00:09:37.060
Now,

00:09:37.060 --> 00:09:43.810
how do I solve for X, Y, Z, and
W?

00:09:43.900 --> 00:09:47.100
Can I set up a system of
equations that would tell me

00:09:47.100 --> 00:09:50.160
what these four numbers would
have to be?

00:09:50.160 --> 00:09:51.160
Yeah?

00:09:51.440 --> 00:09:54.040
Doesn't it equal the identity?

00:09:54.040 --> 00:09:55.920
When you multiply it by A.

00:09:55.920 --> 00:09:57.420
Yeah, exactly.

00:09:57.500 --> 00:10:01.340
So what I could do is write out
1,

00:10:01.340 --> 00:10:01.780
5,

00:10:01.780 --> 00:10:04.500
2, 12 multiplied

00:10:09.720 --> 00:10:13.470
by x, y, z, w.

00:10:13.660 --> 00:10:16.480
That product needs to be the
identity matrix,

00:10:16.480 --> 00:10:17.080
1, 0,

00:10:17.080 --> 00:10:18.580
0, 1.

00:10:27.060 --> 00:10:33.060
So what I could do is expand the
left-hand side of this equation,

00:10:33.100 --> 00:10:36.100
and it tells me

00:10:36.450 --> 00:10:40.950
a system of four equations and
four unknowns.

00:10:50.420 --> 00:10:51.560
Right?

00:10:53.380 --> 00:10:58.840
One equation is x plus 5z is
equal to what?

00:11:04.160 --> 00:11:05.120
Yeah,

00:11:05.120 --> 00:11:06.080
1.

00:11:06.080 --> 00:11:10.940
Another equation is 2x plus 12z
is equal to what?

00:11:15.680 --> 00:11:16.680
Yeah,

00:11:16.700 --> 00:11:20.940
0.
So another is y plus 5w is equal

00:11:20.940 --> 00:11:22.020
to 0.

00:11:22.020 --> 00:11:25.620
Another is 2y plus 12w is equal
to 1.

00:11:25.620 --> 00:11:28.520
If I solve these equations for
x, y, z, and w,

00:11:28.520 --> 00:11:30.160
it'll tell me the inverse of it.

00:11:30.160 --> 00:11:32.320
So I could do this.

00:11:32.320 --> 00:11:36.260
And there is nothing unique to 2
by 2 matrices here.

00:11:36.260 --> 00:11:39.120
If I give you a 7 by 7 matrix,

00:11:39.120 --> 00:11:40.500
you could.
I mean, I wouldn't ask you to do

00:11:40.500 --> 00:11:43.020
this, at least not this way.

00:11:43.020 --> 00:11:47.160
But you could make a template
for what its inverse would be,

00:11:47.160 --> 00:11:49.560
which would be a grid of 49
numbers.

00:11:49.560 --> 00:11:51.840
I mean, you would run out of
letters if you tried to assign

00:11:51.840 --> 00:11:54.000
them letters, so you'd have to
index them somehow.

00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:58.480
And you could set up a system of
49 equations and 49 unknowns to

00:11:58.480 --> 00:12:00.560
find the inverse.

00:12:00.560 --> 00:12:03.820
It's not particularly efficient,
but you could do this without

00:12:03.820 --> 00:12:06.520
needing to memorize any
formulas.

00:12:06.520 --> 00:12:08.520
Note, by the way,

00:12:08.520 --> 00:12:11.560
I just thought of an exam
question while I was talking

00:12:11.560 --> 00:12:13.480
about this, so I figured I may
as well tell it to you.

00:12:13.480 --> 00:12:17.060
All the inverse is really asking
us to do

00:12:17.060 --> 00:12:21.560
is write the default or the
standard basis vectors in R2

00:12:21.760 --> 00:12:25.340
as linear combinations of the
columns of the matrix.

00:12:25.340 --> 00:12:28.840
If I find x and z,

00:12:28.840 --> 00:12:31.760
well, that's telling me how much
to take of A's first column and

00:12:31.760 --> 00:12:35.540
A's second column to make 1, 0.

00:12:35.540 --> 00:12:40.790
And if I find y and w,

00:12:40.820 --> 00:12:45.080
that's telling me how much to
take of A's first column and A's

00:12:45.080 --> 00:12:48.060
second column to make 0, 1,
which is the second standard

00:12:48.060 --> 00:12:49.780
basis vector in R2.

00:12:49.780 --> 00:12:53.760
So normally, we use the standard
basis vectors 1, 0, and 0, 1 as

00:12:53.760 --> 00:12:56.300
our building blocks.
And any vector in R2 is

00:12:56.300 --> 00:12:59.020
implicitly a linear combination
of those things.

00:12:59.020 --> 00:13:04.800
Like 5 times this plus 12 times
that is just

00:13:04.800 --> 00:13:05.800
512.

00:13:06.920 --> 00:13:08.600
Here, we're asking for the
opposite.

00:13:08.600 --> 00:13:09.240
We're saying,

00:13:09.240 --> 00:13:11.500
OK, we have some matrix whose
columns are just made up of

00:13:11.500 --> 00:13:12.920
arbitrary numbers,

00:13:12.920 --> 00:13:15.860
how do we combine those to get
the building blocks,

00:13:15.860 --> 00:13:17.440
1, 0, and 0, 1?

00:13:17.440 --> 00:13:20.580
So you can imagine if an exam
question said,

00:13:20.580 --> 00:13:21.580
OK,

00:13:21.700 --> 00:13:24.060
I have a 3 by 3 matrix A,

00:13:24.060 --> 00:13:28.720
and A times 3, 4, 5 is equal to
1, 0, 0.

00:13:28.720 --> 00:13:30.120
What does that tell you?

00:13:30.120 --> 00:13:32.740
It tells you 3, 4, 5 is the
first column of the inverse of

00:13:32.740 --> 00:13:34.990
A, for example.

00:13:39.500 --> 00:13:42.920
By the way, I said 512.

00:13:42.920 --> 00:13:48.460
This past Saturday was the last
Pythagorean triplet day likely

00:13:48.460 --> 00:13:50.280
of our lifetimes, because I
think the next one is in like

00:13:50.280 --> 00:13:51.280
2103.

00:13:52.600 --> 00:13:55.720
Maybe some of you will live till
then.

00:13:55.720 --> 00:14:00.220
But 10 squared plus 24 squared
is 26 squared.

00:14:00.280 --> 00:14:01.280
OK.

00:14:01.780 --> 00:14:02.780
Anyways,

00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:08.220
so I could give you a matrix in
theory of any size, and you have

00:14:08.220 --> 00:14:12.840
the basic understanding of how
you would find its inverse.

00:14:13.680 --> 00:14:16.920
Now, in practice, this is not
how you would find an inverse.

00:14:16.920 --> 00:14:20.000
There's really only one case in
which you need to memorize a

00:14:20.000 --> 00:14:21.000
formula,

00:14:21.140 --> 00:14:24.520
and it's for a 2 by 2 matrix.

00:14:24.520 --> 00:14:24.880
OK,

00:14:24.880 --> 00:14:27.040
the 2 by 2 inverse comes up
often enough that you should

00:14:27.040 --> 00:14:31.540
just know what its formula is,
And I'll tell it to you.

00:14:39.260 --> 00:14:41.540
If your matrix has components A,

00:14:41.540 --> 00:14:41.980
B,

00:14:41.980 --> 00:14:44.230
C, and D,

00:14:44.240 --> 00:14:45.680
the inverse

00:14:45.680 --> 00:14:51.480
involves taking the

00:14:51.480 --> 00:14:52.980
diagonal elements,

00:14:53.080 --> 00:14:54.650
A and D,

00:14:54.650 --> 00:14:56.900
and swapping them,

00:14:57.000 --> 00:15:00.230
turning it to D and A,

00:15:00.230 --> 00:15:03.230
and taking the off-diagonal
elements, B and C,

00:15:04.320 --> 00:15:07.320
and making them negative.

00:15:09.660 --> 00:15:11.320
But that's not all.

00:15:11.320 --> 00:15:16.560
You also multiply all of this by
1 over AD

00:15:16.560 --> 00:15:18.460
minus BC.

00:15:25.340 --> 00:15:29.090
And I'll say, remember this.

00:15:32.760 --> 00:15:33.760
OK?

00:15:35.680 --> 00:15:36.680
Now,

00:15:37.160 --> 00:15:39.220
pretend you don't believe me.
I don't think you think I'm

00:15:39.220 --> 00:15:40.700
lying.
But if you didn't believe me,

00:15:41.520 --> 00:15:44.840
how would you verify that this
is indeed actually the formula

00:15:44.840 --> 00:15:48.400
for the inverse of a 2 by 2
matrix? How would you check this

00:15:48.400 --> 00:15:53.650
if you weren't sure that this is
true?

00:15:54.180 --> 00:15:55.060
Yeah?

00:15:55.060 --> 00:15:57.820
Multiply them together and see
if it equals any matrix.

00:15:57.820 --> 00:15:58.420
Yeah.

00:15:58.420 --> 00:15:59.960
You have a built-in way of
verifying.

00:15:59.960 --> 00:16:01.940
You just multiply A by A
inverse,

00:16:01.940 --> 00:16:04.980
or A inverse by A, either order,
and it should give you the

00:16:04.980 --> 00:16:07.480
identity matrix if I'm telling
you the truth,

00:16:07.480 --> 00:16:08.480
OK?

00:16:08.940 --> 00:16:12.060
So you could apply this to the
matrix

00:16:12.060 --> 00:16:15.480
that we had in the previous
example,

00:16:15.480 --> 00:16:15.860
1,

00:16:15.860 --> 00:16:18.110
5, 2, 12.

00:16:20.460 --> 00:16:21.800
What does it involve?

00:16:21.800 --> 00:16:24.500
We swap the diagonal.
So on the diagonal, instead of 1

00:16:24.500 --> 00:16:29.340
and 12, we have 12 and 1.
And the off diagonals we negate.

00:16:29.340 --> 00:16:33.080
We also have to multiply all of
this by 1 over the product of

00:16:33.080 --> 00:16:35.960
the diagonals minus the product
of the off diagonals.

00:16:35.960 --> 00:16:40.460
So here, that's 12 minus 10,
which is 2.

00:16:43.000 --> 00:16:48.250
So if you were to simplify a
little.

00:16:52.800 --> 00:16:56.200
this 1 half gets multiplied into
every element.

00:16:56.200 --> 00:16:58.260
So this is really the matrix 6,

00:16:58.260 --> 00:16:59.760
negative 1,

00:17:00.060 --> 00:17:01.480
negative 5 halves,

00:17:01.480 --> 00:17:04.480
and positive 1 half.

00:17:09.860 --> 00:17:12.920
So for example, this is telling
me,

00:17:12.920 --> 00:17:18.920
if all you know is that this is
the inverse of A,

00:17:20.080 --> 00:17:26.060
This tells you that 6 times A's
first column minus 1 times A's

00:17:26.060 --> 00:17:30.560
second column is equal to the
vector 1, 0.

00:17:30.920 --> 00:17:33.280
Are we clear on why that is the
case?

00:17:33.280 --> 00:17:36.440
Just from looking at what's on
the page and nothing else,

00:17:36.440 --> 00:17:38.700
we know that whatever A is,

00:17:38.700 --> 00:17:42.480
6 times its first column minus 1
times its second column is equal

00:17:42.480 --> 00:17:45.980
to the vector 1, 0.

00:17:45.980 --> 00:17:50.480
What questions do we have with
that idea?

00:17:51.160 --> 00:17:55.660
Do we see where that's coming
from?

00:17:58.720 --> 00:18:04.500
Now, there's a name for this
denominator

00:18:04.500 --> 00:18:05.500
here.

00:18:08.060 --> 00:18:09.260
Does anyone know what it's
called?

00:18:09.260 --> 00:18:11.560
I mentioned it earlier today.

00:18:11.560 --> 00:18:14.060
What is this denominator here
called? Is it the determinant?

00:18:14.060 --> 00:18:18.380
Yeah, this is called the
determinant,

00:18:23.500 --> 00:18:25.660
written dete.

00:18:33.620 --> 00:18:38.120
OK, so let's talk about what
that is.

00:18:44.400 --> 00:18:49.920
So if A is a 2 by 2 matrix,

00:18:49.920 --> 00:18:52.920
the determinant of A

00:18:53.190 --> 00:18:55.080
is AD minus BC,

00:18:55.080 --> 00:18:58.080
which is a scalar.

00:18:58.900 --> 00:19:01.800
So you can think of the
determinant as a function that

00:19:01.800 --> 00:19:04.600
takes in a matrix and returns a
single number.

00:19:04.600 --> 00:19:08.920
And somehow that number
describes something about the

00:19:08.920 --> 00:19:11.960
matrix, including whether or not
it is invertible.

00:19:11.960 --> 00:19:14.960
Because what would happen,

00:19:15.900 --> 00:19:16.900
actually,

00:19:17.500 --> 00:19:19.680
instead of just asking
hypothetically,

00:19:19.680 --> 00:19:22.340
why don't I give you another
matrix?

00:19:22.340 --> 00:19:22.740
1,

00:19:22.740 --> 00:19:23.200
6,

00:19:23.200 --> 00:19:24.700
2, 12.

00:19:25.600 --> 00:19:30.100
What is the determinant of this
matrix?

00:19:34.380 --> 00:19:36.380
What is the determinant of 1,

00:19:36.380 --> 00:19:36.920
6,

00:19:36.920 --> 00:19:38.420
2, 12?

00:19:44.180 --> 00:19:45.180
Yeah.

00:19:45.500 --> 00:19:46.500
0.

00:19:51.000 --> 00:19:55.500
And what do you also notice
about the matrix?

00:19:57.820 --> 00:19:59.320
A is?

00:20:01.540 --> 00:20:04.540
It's determinant to 0.

00:20:05.580 --> 00:20:06.580
Yeah.

00:20:07.360 --> 00:20:08.360
Yeah,

00:20:08.720 --> 00:20:10.300
rank is 1, meaning,

00:20:10.300 --> 00:20:12.720
in the context of the stuff
we're talking about here,

00:20:12.720 --> 00:20:14.180
is it invertible?

00:20:14.180 --> 00:20:14.680
No,

00:20:14.680 --> 00:20:17.680
A is not invertible.

00:20:20.660 --> 00:20:22.440
Why is it not invertible?

00:20:22.440 --> 00:20:23.280
Well, it's because,

00:20:23.280 --> 00:20:26.240
if you go back to the list of
equivalent conditions we said

00:20:26.240 --> 00:20:27.180
before,

00:20:27.180 --> 00:20:29.540
remember all of these are
equivalent to each other.

00:20:29.540 --> 00:20:31.960
If any one of them is true,
they're all true.

00:20:31.960 --> 00:20:34.960
Should maybe emphasize that.

00:20:36.900 --> 00:20:42.420
all equivalent for square

00:20:42.420 --> 00:20:44.020
matrices.

00:20:47.160 --> 00:20:50.240
It's not the case that A's
columns are linearly

00:20:50.240 --> 00:20:51.220
independent.

00:20:51.220 --> 00:20:51.680
They're not.

00:20:51.680 --> 00:20:57.380
The second column in this
example here is double

00:20:57.380 --> 00:20:59.630
the first column.

00:20:59.940 --> 00:21:02.320
It's also not the case that the
rows are linearly independent.

00:21:02.320 --> 00:21:05.020
The second row is six times the
first row.

00:21:05.020 --> 00:21:08.160
It's also not the case that the
rank is equal to n.

00:21:08.160 --> 00:21:11.780
The rank here is 1, not 2.

00:21:11.780 --> 00:21:14.340
So it seems like this
determinant, however it is

00:21:14.340 --> 00:21:15.180
defined,

00:21:15.180 --> 00:21:19.680
has something to do with the
invertibility of the matrix.

00:21:20.540 --> 00:21:22.800
Now, this matrix back here,

00:21:22.800 --> 00:21:24.400
what was its determinant?

00:21:24.400 --> 00:21:26.740
Notice the only difference
between this one now that's

00:21:26.740 --> 00:21:29.680
currently on the page and the
one we were just looking at is

00:21:29.680 --> 00:21:32.900
the top right entry is a 5
instead of a 6.

00:21:32.900 --> 00:21:35.140
What is this matrix's
determinant? Matrix is

00:21:35.140 --> 00:21:36.140
determinant.

00:21:37.260 --> 00:21:38.260
2.

00:21:38.420 --> 00:21:41.220
So the question is, what does 2
mean

00:21:41.220 --> 00:21:43.840
in the context of this matrix?

00:21:43.840 --> 00:21:45.340
So remember,

00:21:56.800 --> 00:21:57.780
last class,

00:21:57.780 --> 00:22:00.060
and let me use that same matrix
that we've just written out

00:22:00.060 --> 00:22:00.580
here,

00:22:00.580 --> 00:22:00.960
1,

00:22:00.960 --> 00:22:03.210
5, 2, 12.

00:22:04.720 --> 00:22:07.720
We've visualized the linear
transformation

00:22:07.870 --> 00:22:12.120
that a matrix performs by, on
the left,

00:22:12.120 --> 00:22:16.740
drawing the vectors 1, 0, and 0,
1,

00:22:16.740 --> 00:22:21.990
which there we called the u1 and
u2.

00:22:25.730 --> 00:22:30.530
And we sort of showed the
parallelogram that was enclosed

00:22:30.530 --> 00:22:32.870
by those standard basis vectors.

00:22:32.870 --> 00:22:36.990
What shape is this that I've
just colored in?

00:22:36.990 --> 00:22:38.290
Not a trick question.

00:22:38.290 --> 00:22:39.430
What shape is that?

00:22:39.430 --> 00:22:39.950
Yeah,

00:22:39.950 --> 00:22:42.200
it's a square.

00:22:45.310 --> 00:22:48.090
I would draw this square on the
left and then on the right I

00:22:48.090 --> 00:22:51.840
wouldn't draw a square, where
instead, what was I drawing?

00:22:52.070 --> 00:22:54.530
To show the effect of this
linear transformation, what did

00:22:54.530 --> 00:22:58.280
I draw on the right?

00:23:05.150 --> 00:23:06.310
Didn't draw a square.

00:23:06.310 --> 00:23:07.550
What did I draw?

00:23:07.550 --> 00:23:09.490
It was in orange.

00:23:09.490 --> 00:23:14.030
But what did we draw on the
right?

00:23:14.030 --> 00:23:16.280
Is it vectors?

00:23:17.510 --> 00:23:20.110
There's multiple vectors that I
would draw on the right.

00:23:20.110 --> 00:23:23.890
We were trying to visualize the
effect of multiplying vectors by

00:23:23.890 --> 00:23:24.890
a.

00:23:25.170 --> 00:23:28.830
And we were trying to do this in
the context of the two standard

00:23:28.830 --> 00:23:30.950
building blocks, 1, 0, and 0, 1.

00:23:30.950 --> 00:23:34.070
So what was I drawing on the
right before?

00:23:34.070 --> 00:23:36.310
Like a rectangle or a triangle?

00:23:36.310 --> 00:23:39.230
Was it a triangle? Sometimes a
rectangle, sometimes a diagonal

00:23:39.230 --> 00:23:40.030
shape,

00:23:40.030 --> 00:23:42.510
or sorry, like a diamond shape.
But where were those coming

00:23:42.510 --> 00:23:43.510
from?

00:23:45.090 --> 00:23:46.250
How did I do,

00:23:46.250 --> 00:23:46.610
like,

00:23:46.610 --> 00:23:49.550
those diamonds and rectangles,

00:23:49.550 --> 00:23:52.550
how are they defined?

00:23:59.230 --> 00:24:01.750
remember what

00:24:01.750 --> 00:24:07.150
we did is we multiplied

00:24:07.150 --> 00:24:12.190
both of these basis vectors by
the matrix A.

00:24:13.930 --> 00:24:17.010
So instead of drawing 1, 0,

00:24:17.010 --> 00:24:22.650
I would draw A times 1, 0, which
is just the vector 1, 2.

00:24:22.650 --> 00:24:25.850
Remember, A times 1, 0 is just
the first column of A.

00:24:25.850 --> 00:24:29.130
And A times 0, 1 is the second
column of A.

00:24:29.130 --> 00:24:32.590
So I would draw A times 1, 0,

00:24:32.590 --> 00:24:33.990
and then A.

00:24:33.990 --> 00:24:35.530
OK, well, it's not quite going
to look like that.

00:24:35.530 --> 00:24:40.780
It's going to be more like that.

00:24:43.570 --> 00:24:45.490
A times 0, 1,

00:24:45.490 --> 00:24:50.590
which is the second column of A,
which is 5, 12.

00:24:50.590 --> 00:24:54.130
Does this now kind of seem
familiar?

00:24:54.130 --> 00:24:57.950
OK, this is an important idea,
so make sure to review it.

00:24:57.950 --> 00:24:59.890
In addition to just drawing
these,

00:24:59.890 --> 00:25:00.750
though,

00:25:00.750 --> 00:25:04.890
I also colored in the
parallelogram

00:25:06.550 --> 00:25:09.550
that these vectors enclose.
OK,

00:25:09.850 --> 00:25:14.350
actually, it would be a little
taller than that.

00:25:16.170 --> 00:25:18.420
Something like this,

00:25:21.390 --> 00:25:22.390
this.

00:25:25.790 --> 00:25:28.950
And this idea of two vectors and
their sum and difference in

00:25:28.950 --> 00:25:31.770
closing of parallelogram is one
that you saw in earlier

00:25:31.770 --> 00:25:32.770
homeworks.

00:25:33.270 --> 00:25:34.270
OK?

00:25:35.010 --> 00:25:38.950
The determinant of a matrix
tells you

00:25:40.110 --> 00:25:43.860
the area of that parallelogram,

00:25:44.590 --> 00:25:54.510
or

00:25:54.510 --> 00:25:56.870
more precisely, the absolute
value of the determinant,

00:25:56.870 --> 00:26:00.620
because the determinant could be
positive or negative.

00:26:00.650 --> 00:26:05.150
Now, this square that we started
with, What was its area?

00:26:05.450 --> 00:26:08.250
The square on the left.
What's its area?

00:26:08.250 --> 00:26:09.250
Yeah?

00:26:09.430 --> 00:26:10.270
1.

00:26:10.270 --> 00:26:11.270
OK.

00:26:13.450 --> 00:26:15.150
This parallelogram on the right,

00:26:15.150 --> 00:26:18.900
its determinant is the absolute
value of,

00:26:19.710 --> 00:26:20.230
sorry,

00:26:20.230 --> 00:26:22.370
its area is the absolute value
of the determinant of the

00:26:22.370 --> 00:26:23.370
matrix.

00:26:23.710 --> 00:26:24.450
Now the thing is,

00:26:24.450 --> 00:26:30.070
this area interpretation works
in R2, or when you're dealing

00:26:30.070 --> 00:26:32.650
with a 2 by 2 matrix.
But what if you're dealing with

00:26:32.650 --> 00:26:34.900
a three-by-three matrix?

00:26:39.270 --> 00:26:42.270
Let's come down here.

00:26:43.430 --> 00:26:46.070
That's not what I'm looking for.

00:26:46.070 --> 00:26:48.320
Here we go.

00:26:50.690 --> 00:26:53.110
With a three-by-three matrix,
it's not colored in for some

00:26:53.110 --> 00:26:54.050
reason.
I'll fix that.

00:26:54.050 --> 00:26:58.870
But instead, you have three
standard basis vectors,

00:26:59.450 --> 00:27:02.790
and they don't form a square
with area one,

00:27:02.790 --> 00:27:05.070
what do they form on the left?
The shape on the left, what is

00:27:05.070 --> 00:27:06.070
it?

00:27:06.390 --> 00:27:07.130
Yeah,

00:27:07.130 --> 00:27:09.970
it's a cube with volume one,

00:27:09.970 --> 00:27:10.970
okay?

00:27:11.130 --> 00:27:12.770
Now on the right,

00:27:12.770 --> 00:27:16.710
what I've shown for some matrix
K, we've called it,

00:27:16.710 --> 00:27:20.090
I've shown the result of
transforming the three standard

00:27:20.090 --> 00:27:22.930
basis vectors in R3,

00:27:22.930 --> 00:27:24.170
1, 0, 0,

00:27:24.170 --> 00:27:27.870
0, 1, 0, and 0, 0, 1.
I've multiplied them all by the

00:27:27.870 --> 00:27:28.570
matrix,

00:27:28.570 --> 00:27:30.530
and we're looking at the results
here.

00:27:30.530 --> 00:27:35.330
And if I were to draw in the
shape that they

00:27:35.330 --> 00:27:36.850
enclose,

00:27:38.370 --> 00:27:40.490
it's not a parallelogram,

00:27:40.490 --> 00:27:42.010
because that's a 2D thing,

00:27:42.010 --> 00:27:45.010
but what is it?

00:27:46.030 --> 00:27:48.130
What shape would these things
enclose? Does anyone know what

00:27:48.130 --> 00:27:49.980
it's called? It's kind of a
weird name.

00:27:49.980 --> 00:27:52.930
Yeah? It would be a rectangular
prism.

00:27:52.930 --> 00:27:55.510
Close.
That's like a box where all the

00:27:55.510 --> 00:27:56.830
edges are straight.

00:27:56.830 --> 00:27:58.330
But here,

00:27:58.350 --> 00:28:02.100
the edges can be slanted.

00:28:05.570 --> 00:28:08.790
It's called the parallelepiped
weird name.

00:28:12.930 --> 00:28:16.390
And the determinant of the
matrix,

00:28:16.390 --> 00:28:21.210
or its absolute value, tells you
the volume of this slanted box,

00:28:24.080 --> 00:28:27.080
just like the determinant of the
identity matrix

00:28:27.080 --> 00:28:30.370
would tell you the volume of
this box,

00:28:30.370 --> 00:28:32.620
which is what?

00:28:37.570 --> 00:28:43.390
The matrix was

00:28:43.390 --> 00:28:44.890
called K.

00:28:45.010 --> 00:28:46.630
So in general,

00:28:46.630 --> 00:28:48.630
what does the determinant tell
you?

00:28:48.630 --> 00:28:52.450
It tells you the n-dimensional
volume

00:28:55.050 --> 00:28:58.470
enclosed by the standard basis
vectors

00:28:58.470 --> 00:29:01.070
after they are transformed by
the matrix.

00:29:01.070 --> 00:29:03.210
What do I mean by n-dimensional
volume?

00:29:03.210 --> 00:29:03.690
Well,

00:29:03.690 --> 00:29:06.430
in 2D, that was area.
In 3D, that was volume.

00:29:06.430 --> 00:29:07.870
In R4,

00:29:07.870 --> 00:29:11.620
it's like whatever the version
of volume is in R4,

00:29:11.810 --> 00:29:13.970
the four-dimensional volume.

00:29:13.970 --> 00:29:17.410
How much space in 4D does it
take up?

00:29:17.410 --> 00:29:20.190
Kind of a confusing concept to
think about because, again, it's

00:29:20.190 --> 00:29:21.570
not something we can visualize.

00:29:21.570 --> 00:29:23.990
But in general, the determinant
is measuring

00:29:23.990 --> 00:29:26.240
the n-dimensional volume.

00:29:27.550 --> 00:29:31.210
An example of not n-dimensional
volume would be surface area in

00:29:31.210 --> 00:29:32.210
3D.

00:29:32.630 --> 00:29:35.290
Surface area is not the same as
the amount of space a thing

00:29:35.290 --> 00:29:36.790
takes up.
It's like how much,

00:29:37.230 --> 00:29:37.750
you know,

00:29:37.750 --> 00:29:40.310
like what is the perimeter kind
of an object.

00:29:40.310 --> 00:29:41.230
Yeah?

00:29:41.230 --> 00:29:44.890
So based on what the volume like
question says,

00:29:44.890 --> 00:29:49.390
so the volume is independent of
what the original vector is?

00:29:50.890 --> 00:29:54.640
Is it only the determinant?

00:29:55.070 --> 00:29:56.490
So the determinant,

00:29:56.490 --> 00:30:00.110
you should think of it as the
volume of the box that results

00:30:00.110 --> 00:30:02.850
from transforming the three
standard basis vectors.

00:30:02.850 --> 00:30:04.830
Now, of all three of these
vectors on the left, if I

00:30:04.830 --> 00:30:06.510
doubled all of them,

00:30:06.510 --> 00:30:08.230
that would have some other
effect on the volume here.

00:30:08.230 --> 00:30:10.290
It would actually multiply it by
8,

00:30:10.290 --> 00:30:13.170
because you're doubling each.
Like if you take a box in 3D and

00:30:13.170 --> 00:30:16.270
stretch it lengthwise,
widthwise, and heightwise by 2,

00:30:16.970 --> 00:30:19.590
you increase its volume by a
factor of 8.

00:30:19.590 --> 00:30:22.190
Yeah, but do you change the
determinant?

00:30:22.190 --> 00:30:24.330
Well, no, the determinant is
defined this very particular

00:30:24.330 --> 00:30:24.930
way.

00:30:24.930 --> 00:30:28.790
It's the volume of the box that
results from transforming the

00:30:28.790 --> 00:30:30.450
standard basis vectors.

00:30:30.450 --> 00:30:31.450
Yeah.

00:31:05.370 --> 00:31:08.850
Now, the actual value of the
determinant is not something we

00:31:08.850 --> 00:31:11.790
care that much about in this
class,

00:31:11.790 --> 00:31:13.270
care that much about.

00:31:13.270 --> 00:31:17.710
Really, all I care about is
whether or not the determinant

00:31:17.710 --> 00:31:19.210
is 0.

00:31:19.350 --> 00:31:23.330
Now, what would a determinant of
0 mean?

00:31:23.330 --> 00:31:24.630
Let's go back to this example.

00:31:24.630 --> 00:31:27.650
This matrix does not have a
determinant of 0.

00:31:27.650 --> 00:31:30.090
But can you give me a matrix
that does have a determinant of

00:31:30.090 --> 00:31:31.090
0?

00:31:34.370 --> 00:31:38.450
You can just replace the 5 with
the 6 in the parametrics.

00:31:38.450 --> 00:31:42.950
What if the determinant is 0?

00:31:45.830 --> 00:31:49.990
We already saw an example, 1, 6,
2, 12.

00:31:49.990 --> 00:31:53.030
If I want to draw that
equivalent figure,

00:31:53.030 --> 00:31:57.170
I have my box on the left
enclosed, or my square on the

00:31:57.170 --> 00:32:01.010
left enclosed by 1, 0, and 0, 1.

00:32:01.010 --> 00:32:03.310
It had an area of 1.

00:32:03.310 --> 00:32:06.230
But on the right,

00:32:06.230 --> 00:32:10.690
when I multiply

00:32:10.690 --> 00:32:16.510
A

00:32:16.510 --> 00:32:20.910
by the first standard basis
vector 1, 0, I get 1, 2.

00:32:21.410 --> 00:32:26.660
And when I multiply a by the
second standard basis vector,

00:32:29.310 --> 00:32:31.050
I get 6, 12.
I mean, this should be six times

00:32:31.050 --> 00:32:33.250
as long, but you get the idea.

00:32:33.250 --> 00:32:36.310
And what's the area of the
parallelogram enclosed by those

00:32:36.310 --> 00:32:37.310
vectors?

00:32:40.050 --> 00:32:42.910
0, because there isn't a
parallelogram enclosed by them,

00:32:42.910 --> 00:32:45.910
because they live on the same
line.

00:32:45.910 --> 00:32:47.410
OK? OK,

00:32:48.210 --> 00:32:51.790
no enclosed

00:32:51.790 --> 00:32:54.910
parallelogram.

00:32:58.670 --> 00:33:00.770
Determinant is 0.

00:33:00.770 --> 00:33:03.970
But why is the determinant 0?
Why is there no enclosed

00:33:03.970 --> 00:33:04.970
parallelogram?

00:33:05.050 --> 00:33:08.210
Well, it's because these vectors
are linearly dependent.

00:33:08.210 --> 00:33:10.070
They live on the same line.

00:33:10.070 --> 00:33:12.870
But if vectors are linearly
dependent,

00:33:12.870 --> 00:33:13.870
Well,

00:33:14.410 --> 00:33:14.790
then,

00:33:14.790 --> 00:33:18.090
a matrix that is made up of
those vectors in its columns is

00:33:18.090 --> 00:33:19.590
not invertible.

00:33:24.050 --> 00:33:24.990
OK?

00:33:24.990 --> 00:33:25.570
Yeah?

00:33:25.570 --> 00:33:29.530
So is the determinant also the
factor by how much the area

00:33:29.530 --> 00:33:31.330
changes of the original?

00:33:31.330 --> 00:33:32.890
Indeed.
Yeah, that's exactly what it is,

00:33:32.890 --> 00:33:35.250
because the starting area is 1.

00:33:35.250 --> 00:33:41.250
So if it was 2, then the second
one would be 2.

00:33:42.010 --> 00:33:45.610
If the determinant was two,
let's look at this exact

00:33:45.610 --> 00:33:46.590
example.

00:33:46.590 --> 00:33:48.930
The determinant of this matrix
is two.

00:33:48.930 --> 00:33:51.490
This area is one.
This area is two.

00:33:51.490 --> 00:33:52.490
Yeah?

00:33:54.750 --> 00:33:57.830
So if you change the original,
like the two factors,

00:33:57.830 --> 00:34:00.610
to 2, 0, and 0, 2,

00:34:00.610 --> 00:34:03.430
doesn't the area also change,
but the determinant will stay

00:34:03.430 --> 00:34:07.930
the same? The determinant is a
fixed number

00:34:08.290 --> 00:34:13.570
that is defined only involving
the transformation

00:34:13.570 --> 00:34:16.570
of 1, 0, and 0, 1.

00:34:16.570 --> 00:34:20.730
If you were to create a new
shape enclosed by 2, 0, and 0,

00:34:20.730 --> 00:34:23.850
2, and the transformations of
them,

00:34:23.850 --> 00:34:26.590
that would be some shape with
some area, but its area is not

00:34:26.590 --> 00:34:28.930
the determinant of this matrix.
It's related to the determinant

00:34:28.930 --> 00:34:30.870
of the area of this matrix,

00:34:30.870 --> 00:34:32.870
or the determinant of this
matrix, but it's not the same.

00:34:32.870 --> 00:34:34.370
Oh, OK.

00:34:34.730 --> 00:34:37.990
I'll comment on that in just a
moment.

00:34:37.990 --> 00:34:42.750
The bigger idea here is that the
determinant being 0 tells you

00:34:42.750 --> 00:34:46.510
that the standard basis vectors,
after being transformed by this

00:34:46.510 --> 00:34:47.490
matrix,

00:34:47.490 --> 00:34:50.910
no longer enclose an
n-dimensional shape.

00:34:51.770 --> 00:34:52.710
Like here,

00:34:52.710 --> 00:34:54.390
we have a 2 by 2 matrix.

00:34:54.390 --> 00:34:55.970
Before the transformation,

00:34:55.970 --> 00:34:58.550
the standard basis vectors
enclose the square.

00:34:58.550 --> 00:35:00.990
If the columns were linearly
independent, we'd expect them to

00:35:00.990 --> 00:35:03.450
still enclose some
parallelogram.

00:35:03.670 --> 00:35:06.030
But here, the columns are
linearly dependent, so they live

00:35:06.030 --> 00:35:07.690
on a line.
So there's no area, so the

00:35:07.690 --> 00:35:09.110
determinant is 0.

00:35:09.110 --> 00:35:12.430
But the fact that the column
vectors live on a line

00:35:12.430 --> 00:35:14.950
means they are not linearly
independent, which means the

00:35:14.950 --> 00:35:17.030
matrix is not invertible.

00:35:17.030 --> 00:35:20.750
What is the equivalent of this

00:35:20.750 --> 00:35:23.850
in the case of three by three
matrices?

00:35:23.850 --> 00:35:25.490
There's a good visual of this
there.

00:35:25.490 --> 00:35:29.530
Put this load,

00:35:34.370 --> 00:35:36.620
put that again.

00:35:36.670 --> 00:35:38.830
Here we go.

00:35:38.830 --> 00:35:39.830
Here,

00:35:40.110 --> 00:35:42.090
I've given you a matrix L,

00:35:42.090 --> 00:35:46.590
where the second column is just
half of the first column.

00:35:47.050 --> 00:35:50.010
The columns are linearly
dependent.

00:35:50.010 --> 00:35:51.950
So this matrix is not
invertible.

00:35:51.950 --> 00:35:54.450
And what happens when I
transform the three standard

00:35:54.450 --> 00:35:55.750
basis vectors,

00:35:55.750 --> 00:35:58.870
well, they don't all point in
different directions now.

00:35:58.870 --> 00:36:02.530
The three columns of this matrix
are not linearly independent.

00:36:02.530 --> 00:36:04.510
They all live on a plane.

00:36:04.510 --> 00:36:07.350
So the shape that they enclose
also lives on that plane.

00:36:07.350 --> 00:36:09.270
And that plane, what is the
volume of it? Or like that

00:36:09.270 --> 00:36:11.530
shape, what is its volume?

00:36:11.530 --> 00:36:11.910
Zero.

00:36:11.910 --> 00:36:13.110
This shape doesn't have a
volume.

00:36:13.110 --> 00:36:14.330
It has an area,

00:36:14.330 --> 00:36:15.410
but that's not what we're
asking.

00:36:15.410 --> 00:36:18.410
We're asking for its
n-dimensional volume,

00:36:19.130 --> 00:36:20.630
OK? OK.

00:36:21.510 --> 00:36:26.760
So this idea scales up to higher
dimensions as well.

00:36:31.990 --> 00:36:36.510
Here, the determinant of L is 0
because

00:36:36.510 --> 00:36:40.190
enclosed shape

00:36:42.450 --> 00:36:44.700
has no volume.

00:36:46.610 --> 00:36:47.410
OK.

00:36:47.410 --> 00:36:50.790
So are we clear on why the
determinant of this matrix L is

00:36:50.790 --> 00:36:56.730
0 and the various ways to
interpret that? Tells you

00:36:56.730 --> 00:36:59.590
this matrix is not invertible.

00:36:59.590 --> 00:37:03.310
It tells you the volume of the
box enclosed

00:37:03.310 --> 00:37:06.490
by the standard basis vectors.
Once they have been transformed

00:37:06.490 --> 00:37:10.470
by the matrix, the volume of
that box is 0.

00:37:10.470 --> 00:37:12.430
And then it also means all the
other things that come with

00:37:12.430 --> 00:37:13.430
non-invertibility.

00:37:13.750 --> 00:37:15.490
Columns are linearly dependent.

00:37:15.490 --> 00:37:18.110
Rows are linearly dependent.
Null space is more than just the

00:37:18.110 --> 00:37:19.610
0 vector.

00:37:19.990 --> 00:37:24.450
So if that bottom right one was
a 0 and they're on the same

00:37:24.450 --> 00:37:25.610
line,

00:37:26.470 --> 00:37:28.990
the determinant would also be 0?
Correct.

00:37:28.990 --> 00:37:30.710
Let's take the most extreme
example.

00:37:30.710 --> 00:37:35.110
What if all numbers in the
matrix are just 1?

00:37:35.110 --> 00:37:39.470
Then the column space of this
matrix would just be a line.

00:37:39.470 --> 00:37:41.530
Not only would there not be
volume, there wouldn't be even

00:37:41.530 --> 00:37:42.590
an area enclosed.

00:37:42.590 --> 00:37:44.090
They would all live on a line.

00:37:44.090 --> 00:37:46.340
Determinant's still 0.
Yeah.

00:37:46.410 --> 00:37:47.110
So like,

00:37:47.110 --> 00:37:49.570
the determinant being 0 just
tells you the matrix's rank

00:37:49.570 --> 00:37:51.030
isn't 3,

00:37:51.030 --> 00:37:52.750
but it could be 2, 1, or 0.

00:37:52.750 --> 00:37:55.210
And they would all still have a
determinant of 0.

00:37:55.210 --> 00:37:57.590
It's a good point.

00:37:57.590 --> 00:38:00.510
So all you know by a matrix's
determinant being 0 is that its

00:38:00.510 --> 00:38:03.350
rank isn't as high as possible.
But what is its actual rank? We

00:38:03.350 --> 00:38:04.690
don't know.

00:38:04.690 --> 00:38:05.010
Yeah,

00:38:05.010 --> 00:38:06.010
good.

00:38:06.710 --> 00:38:11.210
What other questions do we have?

00:38:11.670 --> 00:38:12.670
Okay,

00:38:13.350 --> 00:38:17.100
some other thoughts on the
determinant.

00:38:24.770 --> 00:38:30.070
If you swap two rows,

00:38:30.070 --> 00:38:34.050
the determinant is multiplied

00:38:34.050 --> 00:38:37.990
by what?

00:38:41.370 --> 00:38:44.690
Let's test it out.
1,

00:38:44.950 --> 00:38:45.430
5,

00:38:45.430 --> 00:38:50.650
2, 12 versus 2, 12, 1, 5.

00:38:50.650 --> 00:38:54.400
What's the determinant of the
matrix on the left?

00:38:59.270 --> 00:39:00.270
2.

00:39:00.490 --> 00:39:02.670
What's the determinant of the
matrix on the right?

00:39:02.670 --> 00:39:04.050
Negative 2.

00:39:04.050 --> 00:39:07.510
So swapping rows multiplies the
determinant by negative 1.

00:39:07.510 --> 00:39:09.910
And swapping columns also
multiplies the determinant by

00:39:09.910 --> 00:39:11.410
negative 1.
Notice,

00:39:11.630 --> 00:39:15.390
if I go back here,

00:39:16.390 --> 00:39:19.050
I would draw the same shape

00:39:19.050 --> 00:39:23.050
even if I swapped the order of
the columns.

00:39:23.050 --> 00:39:26.350
Like if 512 was the first column
and 12 is the second column,

00:39:26.350 --> 00:39:28.310
the shape would look the same.
I would just have labeled it

00:39:28.310 --> 00:39:29.310
differently.

00:39:30.070 --> 00:39:33.610
Like this would say a times u2,

00:39:33.610 --> 00:39:37.350
and this would say a times u1.
It would be the same shape.

00:39:37.350 --> 00:39:38.690
Do we agree with that?

00:39:38.690 --> 00:39:41.190
OK, so the area of the enclosed
shape should be the same,

00:39:41.190 --> 00:39:44.830
but the determinants are
negative 1 times each other in

00:39:44.830 --> 00:39:48.650
those cases, which is why we
take the absolute value when

00:39:48.650 --> 00:39:53.150
measuring the area of the shape.

00:39:53.730 --> 00:39:55.690
So when you multiply by,

00:39:55.690 --> 00:39:58.130
or when you swap two rows, the
determinant gets multiplied by

00:39:58.130 --> 00:39:59.450
negative 1.

00:39:59.450 --> 00:40:02.190
When you swap two columns,

00:40:02.190 --> 00:40:05.190
the determinant gets multiplied
by negative 1.

00:40:07.070 --> 00:40:08.590
And then finally,

00:40:08.590 --> 00:40:11.590
there's one more property.

00:40:13.450 --> 00:40:17.950
If you multiply a row or a
column

00:40:17.950 --> 00:40:21.910
by the scalar c,

00:40:23.450 --> 00:40:27.200
what does the determinant get
multiplied by?

00:40:28.570 --> 00:40:30.250
If you had to guess.

00:40:30.250 --> 00:40:33.170
If I multiply just one column by
c,

00:40:33.170 --> 00:40:36.050
or just one row by c,

00:40:36.050 --> 00:40:38.650
what do you think happens?

00:40:38.650 --> 00:40:40.950
Yeah? We can multiply by c,
right? The determinant gets

00:40:40.950 --> 00:40:42.610
multiplied by c.

00:40:42.610 --> 00:40:45.470
What that means is if you
multiply a whole matrix by c,

00:40:45.470 --> 00:40:47.930
what does the determinant get
multiplied by?

00:40:47.930 --> 00:40:49.350
Yeah? c squared.

00:40:49.350 --> 00:40:51.210
c to the power of n in general.

00:40:51.210 --> 00:40:52.010
Yeah.

00:40:52.010 --> 00:40:52.390
OK?

00:40:52.390 --> 00:40:53.950
And this is really just a
consequence of what I said

00:40:53.950 --> 00:40:54.690
before.

00:40:54.690 --> 00:40:57.230
Imagine a 1 by 1 by 1 cube,

00:40:57.230 --> 00:40:59.190
and you double its length.

00:40:59.190 --> 00:41:01.250
Well, then its volume gets
doubled.

00:41:01.250 --> 00:41:04.210
But if you double its length,
width, and height, its volume

00:41:04.210 --> 00:41:07.190
gets multiplied by 8.

00:41:07.190 --> 00:41:11.110
By going from a 1 by 1 by 1
cubed to a 2 by 2 by 2 cubed,

00:41:11.110 --> 00:41:14.350
the volume doesn't double, it
increases by 8 times.

00:41:14.350 --> 00:41:15.810
So if you think of the
determinant as being this

00:41:15.810 --> 00:41:16.810
volume,

00:41:16.850 --> 00:41:20.750
changing just one of the columns
scales up the volume.

00:41:20.750 --> 00:41:25.250
So changing all of its columns
scales up the volume n times.

00:41:26.990 --> 00:41:27.990
OK,

00:41:29.350 --> 00:41:29.790
and

00:41:29.790 --> 00:41:36.930
what

00:41:36.930 --> 00:41:40.680
is the determinant of the
identity matrix?

00:41:46.710 --> 00:41:50.430
Should just be 1 because the
area of the box or the n

00:41:50.430 --> 00:41:53.410
dimensional volume of the box
enclosed by the standard basis

00:41:53.410 --> 00:41:54.410
vectors.

00:41:54.490 --> 00:41:56.430
When you transform by the
identity matrix, it doesn't

00:41:56.430 --> 00:41:57.250
change them.

00:41:57.250 --> 00:42:01.450
So it's really just the volume
of this cube

00:42:01.450 --> 00:42:04.590
or the area of that square,
which is 1 in all of those

00:42:04.590 --> 00:42:05.590
cases.

00:42:06.390 --> 00:42:08.850
Now, the reason I'm writing all
of this out is because you can

00:42:08.850 --> 00:42:11.650
sort of this is more abstract
than we will get into.

00:42:11.650 --> 00:42:16.590
But the determinant is the only
operation that satisfies all of

00:42:16.590 --> 00:42:17.850
these properties.

00:42:17.850 --> 00:42:21.570
So like just with these
properties alone, So you can

00:42:21.570 --> 00:42:25.630
find that the determinant
formula that we found for two by

00:42:25.630 --> 00:42:27.970
two matrices is the only
possible formula that matches

00:42:27.970 --> 00:42:29.670
all of these things.

00:42:29.670 --> 00:42:33.420
So the determinant is uniquely
defined by these things.

00:42:49.850 --> 00:42:52.990
There's one other property I'll
state, not because we'll need it

00:42:52.990 --> 00:42:55.230
now, but because it'll come up
later,

00:42:55.230 --> 00:42:58.650
is that the determinant of a
matrix

00:42:58.650 --> 00:43:03.150
is also equal to the determinant
of the transpose.

00:43:06.430 --> 00:43:09.250
Now, in the context of rows and
columns being linearly

00:43:09.250 --> 00:43:11.410
independent, this sort of makes
sense.

00:43:11.410 --> 00:43:14.070
If the rows are not linearly
independent,

00:43:14.070 --> 00:43:16.390
although the determinant should
be 0,

00:43:16.390 --> 00:43:18.090
that should also mean the
columns are not linearly

00:43:18.090 --> 00:43:20.650
independent.
And so we've seen that a and a

00:43:20.650 --> 00:43:23.410
transpose have the same rank So
they should also both be

00:43:23.410 --> 00:43:25.570
invertible at the same time and
not be invertible at the same

00:43:25.570 --> 00:43:29.950
time So it makes sense that the
determinants are the same

00:43:29.950 --> 00:43:34.450
Okay, what I'll say is that
there exists a

00:43:37.290 --> 00:43:40.290
Three by three determinant
formula

00:43:43.630 --> 00:43:46.250
Which I'll have you practice
once or twice in a homework or

00:43:46.250 --> 00:43:47.110
lab

00:43:47.110 --> 00:43:50.070
But it's like not the most
important thing in the world.

00:43:50.070 --> 00:43:53.070
It's defined this way.

00:43:54.450 --> 00:43:57.310
And if you take a closer look at
the notes, it shows how to

00:43:57.310 --> 00:44:00.010
extend this to the determinant
of a four by four or five by

00:44:00.010 --> 00:44:00.770
five.

00:44:00.770 --> 00:44:04.520
Again, not really the point
because we don't really care

00:44:12.170 --> 00:44:14.230
about the value of the
determinant in general.

00:44:14.230 --> 00:44:16.170
We mostly just care if it's zero
or not.

00:44:16.170 --> 00:44:18.670
But sometimes, to check if it's
zero or not, you may need to use

00:44:18.670 --> 00:44:20.170
this formula.

00:44:20.270 --> 00:44:22.490
OK, so what this is showing you
is how you would calculate the

00:44:22.490 --> 00:44:24.610
determinant of a 3 by 3 matrix.

00:44:24.610 --> 00:44:27.890
And the way you think about it
is that you break it up into

00:44:27.890 --> 00:44:31.410
three 2 by 2 determinants and
then stitch them together.

00:44:31.410 --> 00:44:35.590
So it's A times the determinant
of the matrix you get when you

00:44:35.590 --> 00:44:38.290
erase the row and column A is
in.

00:44:38.290 --> 00:44:40.730
So it's A times the determinant
of whatever's left over right

00:44:40.730 --> 00:44:43.510
now, which is the matrix EFHI.

00:44:43.510 --> 00:44:48.430
So it'd be EI minus FH.
then it's B times whatever's

00:44:48.430 --> 00:44:52.090
left over if you delete the row
and column that B is in.

00:44:52.090 --> 00:44:56.250
So you have D F G I and
hopefully the coloring sort of

00:44:56.250 --> 00:44:58.950
implies where that's coming
from.

00:44:58.950 --> 00:44:59.330
Plus,

00:44:59.330 --> 00:45:01.370
so these signs are alternating,

00:45:01.370 --> 00:45:04.390
C times the determinant of
whatever's left over when you

00:45:04.390 --> 00:45:06.410
delete the row and column C is
in.

00:45:06.410 --> 00:45:11.290
So the determinant of D E G H.

00:45:11.290 --> 00:45:14.750
Okay again not super
enlightening but you should

00:45:14.750 --> 00:45:16.150
practice it once or twice.

00:45:16.150 --> 00:45:20.130
And we will sort of have you do
that in upcoming lives and

00:45:20.130 --> 00:45:22.930
homeworks, but not the most
important thing in the world to

00:45:22.930 --> 00:45:23.930
memorize.

00:45:24.010 --> 00:45:26.290
Is that related to the cross
product at all? Because that's

00:45:26.290 --> 00:45:28.070
exactly the formula.

00:45:28.070 --> 00:45:29.510
It is.

00:45:29.510 --> 00:45:31.010
It is.

00:45:31.090 --> 00:45:31.470
Now,

00:45:31.470 --> 00:45:32.230
the thing is,

00:45:32.230 --> 00:45:34.010
the cross product, of course,

00:45:34.010 --> 00:45:36.270
only involves two vectors in R3.

00:45:36.270 --> 00:45:39.110
So you have to do this weird
trick to make this formula work

00:45:39.110 --> 00:45:41.530
with the cross product, which I
don't want to get into because

00:45:41.530 --> 00:45:43.910
it uses the physics
interpretation of a vector, like

00:45:43.910 --> 00:45:46.470
writing I, J, K in one of the
columns or something like that,

00:45:46.470 --> 00:45:47.570
which is a little strange.

00:45:47.570 --> 00:45:52.070
But these ideas are all related,

00:45:52.650 --> 00:45:56.930
which is why if you've seen a
physics example,

00:45:56.930 --> 00:46:00.970
the cross product also measures
the area of some region.

00:46:03.750 --> 00:46:04.750
OK.

00:46:05.030 --> 00:46:06.550
Again, big picture takeaway for
us.

00:46:06.550 --> 00:46:09.590
The real reason we care about
the determinant of the matrix,

00:46:09.590 --> 00:46:14.410
yes, it tells us the volume, the
n-dimensional volume enclosed by

00:46:14.410 --> 00:46:17.430
the standard basis vectors after
they're transformed by the

00:46:17.430 --> 00:46:20.850
matrix We really we just care
about whether the determinant is

00:46:20.850 --> 00:46:25.690
0 or not And if it's 0 it tells
us the matrix is not invertible

00:46:25.690 --> 00:46:31.690
if it's not 0 it tells us the
matrix is invertible

