00:46:58.670 --> 00:47:03.170
A few moments ago, we looked at
the formula for a 2x2 inverse,

00:47:03.170 --> 00:47:06.170
and that's like the one formula
to memorize.

00:47:06.170 --> 00:47:09.730
And before that, we talked about
how you would find the inverse

00:47:09.730 --> 00:47:15.350
of a larger matrix if you had no
other knowledge of a formula.

00:47:15.350 --> 00:47:17.490
You could set up a system of
equations if you really needed

00:47:17.490 --> 00:47:19.670
to do it.

00:47:19.670 --> 00:47:22.910
Now, there exist faster
implementations of inverse

00:47:22.910 --> 00:47:26.660
finding in packages like NumPy.

00:47:26.790 --> 00:47:30.010
But in general, it's actually
rare that we ever need to write

00:47:30.010 --> 00:47:33.750
code or use code that finds an
inverse.

00:47:33.750 --> 00:47:36.390
And here's why.
So last class,

00:47:36.890 --> 00:47:39.410
when I introduced the idea of an
inverse,

00:47:39.410 --> 00:47:42.650
I said that it's useful for one
thing in particular.

00:47:42.650 --> 00:47:44.910
What did I say the inverse was
useful for?

00:47:44.910 --> 00:47:48.430
If I had the inverse of a
matrix, what could I do with it?

00:47:48.430 --> 00:47:52.930
What kind of problem could I
solve with it?

00:47:58.550 --> 00:48:03.050
What kind of problem could I
solve with an inverse?

00:48:04.010 --> 00:48:05.010
Yeah.

00:48:05.130 --> 00:48:06.750
Reversal linear transformation?

00:48:06.750 --> 00:48:08.530
I could reverse a linear
transformation.

00:48:08.530 --> 00:48:10.930
That was the way you should
think about whether a matrix is

00:48:10.930 --> 00:48:12.670
invertible or not.

00:48:12.670 --> 00:48:15.290
But there was a more practical
problem that we could solve

00:48:15.290 --> 00:48:19.250
using the inverse of a matrix if
it existed and we had it.

00:48:19.250 --> 00:48:21.570
What a diagonal projection?

00:48:21.570 --> 00:48:23.030
That's where we're going to come
after this.

00:48:23.030 --> 00:48:25.470
And we're going to use inverses
there.

00:48:25.470 --> 00:48:28.090
But there was an initial problem
I said that you could use

00:48:28.090 --> 00:48:31.090
inverses to help you solve.

00:48:31.090 --> 00:48:34.210
It was for solving a system of
equations.

00:48:34.210 --> 00:48:38.050
Remember, we said Ax equals b.

00:48:38.050 --> 00:48:40.990
This is a system of how many
equations and how many unknowns

00:48:40.990 --> 00:48:44.010
if A is n by n.

00:48:44.010 --> 00:48:46.730
If A is n by n, how many
equations are there and how many

00:48:46.730 --> 00:48:48.980
unknowns are there?

00:48:56.450 --> 00:49:02.410
Yeah? n equations and n

00:49:02.410 --> 00:49:03.430
unknowns.

00:49:05.780 --> 00:49:06.780
Now,

00:49:07.070 --> 00:49:10.070
if A is invertible,

00:49:11.130 --> 00:49:16.470
the unique x

00:49:16.470 --> 00:49:22.210
that satisfies Ax equals B.

00:49:23.350 --> 00:49:26.210
By the way, these variable names
are chosen to illuminate the

00:49:26.210 --> 00:49:29.110
setting where A and B,

00:49:29.110 --> 00:49:31.450
usually we use those for
constants in a question.

00:49:31.450 --> 00:49:33.350
You should think of these as
being given to you in a

00:49:33.350 --> 00:49:34.310
question,

00:49:34.310 --> 00:49:38.510
like these are provided to you
by the data in a question, or

00:49:38.510 --> 00:49:40.550
just otherwise are given to you.

00:49:40.550 --> 00:49:42.330
And x is the unknown,

00:49:42.330 --> 00:49:46.080
which we are solving for.

00:49:47.070 --> 00:49:50.790
If A is invertible, well, then I
can multiply both sides of this

00:49:50.790 --> 00:49:54.390
equation by its inverse.

00:49:56.610 --> 00:49:58.110
And A inverse times A,

00:49:58.110 --> 00:50:01.210
that's just the identity matrix,

00:50:01.210 --> 00:50:06.460
which tells me A inverse times B

00:50:08.830 --> 00:50:12.030
is what I would have to multiply
A by to get B.

00:50:12.030 --> 00:50:16.910
It tells me what the solution to
this system of equations is.

00:50:16.910 --> 00:50:20.150
You should think of this as
conceptually the reason then we

00:50:20.150 --> 00:50:25.400
would need an inverse to solve a
large system of equations.

00:50:25.610 --> 00:50:29.910
If you had A and its inverse,

00:50:29.910 --> 00:50:34.230
well, then you can very quickly
solve any system of equations.

00:50:34.230 --> 00:50:36.470
But where is the issue with
this?

00:50:36.470 --> 00:50:40.930
Pretend all I had was my system
of equations.

00:50:40.930 --> 00:50:44.680
Finding A inverse is actually
really hard to do.

00:50:59.750 --> 00:51:03.110
If you think back to maybe half
an hour ago when I said you

00:51:03.110 --> 00:51:07.610
could find A inverse manually if
you really needed to,

00:51:08.530 --> 00:51:12.710
how did I say you could do it?

00:51:12.710 --> 00:51:14.850
If I just gave you a pencil
paper and as much time as you

00:51:14.850 --> 00:51:15.550
wanted,

00:51:15.550 --> 00:51:17.550
you could find the inverse to
any matrix.

00:51:17.550 --> 00:51:18.610
But what would you have to do?

00:51:18.610 --> 00:51:20.030
Another system of equations.

00:51:20.030 --> 00:51:23.150
Yes, but it's a way larger
system of equations.

00:51:23.150 --> 00:51:25.850
Let's go back here.

00:51:25.850 --> 00:51:28.070
Remember I said, by first
principles, you could find the

00:51:28.070 --> 00:51:29.470
inverse of any matrix.

00:51:29.470 --> 00:51:32.810
Here I had a 2 by 2 matrix.
And I said, OK,

00:51:32.810 --> 00:51:34.070
you don't know the inverse
formula.

00:51:34.070 --> 00:51:36.390
Just figure it out.
Find the inverse of A.

00:51:36.390 --> 00:51:40.190
And what we said was, well, then
the inverse of A had two rows

00:51:40.190 --> 00:51:41.530
and two columns.

00:51:41.530 --> 00:51:43.830
So it has four entries.

00:51:43.830 --> 00:51:46.110
To uniquely find all of them, I
needed a system of four

00:51:46.110 --> 00:51:48.170
equations and four unknowns.

00:51:48.170 --> 00:51:49.170
OK?

00:51:49.210 --> 00:51:53.690
But if all I was looking for is
to find an x where ax equals b

00:51:53.690 --> 00:51:55.350
and x was a vector,

00:51:55.350 --> 00:51:58.850
that's just a system of two
equations and two unknowns.

00:51:58.850 --> 00:52:01.850
If I instead said,

00:52:02.230 --> 00:52:04.430
just solve for x in 1,

00:52:04.430 --> 00:52:06.010
5, 2, 12

00:52:06.010 --> 00:52:09.850
times xy equals 7,

00:52:10.810 --> 00:52:11.810
1,

00:52:12.030 --> 00:52:14.510
that's an easier question.

00:52:14.510 --> 00:52:19.050
So the inverse is like bringing
a sledgehammer when all you need

00:52:19.050 --> 00:52:20.390
is a screwdriver.

00:52:20.390 --> 00:52:23.330
Like, yeah, it could help you
answer this,

00:52:23.330 --> 00:52:26.490
but there's no need to find the
inverse just to answer this.

00:52:26.490 --> 00:52:29.210
You could answer this directly
and more quickly.

00:52:29.210 --> 00:52:30.950
You see what I'm getting at by
that?

00:52:30.950 --> 00:52:32.350
If you had the inverse,

00:52:32.350 --> 00:52:34.890
then sure, you could multiply it
by 7, 1, and it would tell you

00:52:34.890 --> 00:52:36.550
what x and y are supposed to be.

00:52:36.550 --> 00:52:38.350
But if all you need to do is
find x, y, you don't need to

00:52:38.350 --> 00:52:40.150
find the inverse, because that's
way more difficult.

00:52:40.150 --> 00:52:44.870
So this is a system of two
equations and two unknowns, but

00:52:44.870 --> 00:52:47.070
finding the inverse involves
solving a system of four

00:52:47.070 --> 00:52:48.930
equations and four unknowns.

00:52:48.930 --> 00:52:49.930
Similarly,

00:52:53.070 --> 00:52:55.790
this is a system of n equations
and n unknowns.

00:52:55.790 --> 00:52:58.270
But if you wanted to use this
naive approach to find the

00:52:58.270 --> 00:52:59.110
inverse,

00:52:59.110 --> 00:53:02.150
you'd have to solve a system of
n squared equations and n

00:53:02.150 --> 00:53:03.150
squared

00:53:03.410 --> 00:53:05.930
unknowns, because the inverse is
a matrix with n squared things

00:53:05.930 --> 00:53:07.430
in it.

00:53:07.630 --> 00:53:12.880
Do we see why that would make
finding the inverse hard?

00:53:14.370 --> 00:53:18.870
Ax equals b has n equations,

00:53:20.170 --> 00:53:21.670
n unknowns.

00:53:24.690 --> 00:53:28.440
A inverse has n squared
unknowns.

00:53:29.270 --> 00:53:31.450
Now, the inverse is really
powerful because if someone

00:53:31.450 --> 00:53:33.350
already gave it to you,

00:53:33.350 --> 00:53:36.430
it's sort of like you ahead of
time solved every possible

00:53:36.430 --> 00:53:40.180
system of equations and unknowns
involving this matrix.

00:53:40.430 --> 00:53:42.790
No matter what b is,

00:53:42.790 --> 00:53:44.430
if you have a inverse,

00:53:44.430 --> 00:53:47.270
you can really quickly figure
out what x was supposed to be.

00:53:47.270 --> 00:53:49.350
But if I'm just giving you one
b,

00:53:49.350 --> 00:53:52.350
then finding the inverse is
doing an order of magnitude more

00:53:52.350 --> 00:53:54.010
work for no reason,

00:53:54.010 --> 00:53:55.130
which is why,

00:53:55.130 --> 00:53:56.350
in practice,

00:53:56.350 --> 00:53:58.490
and I talk more about this in
the notes,

00:53:58.490 --> 00:54:02.710
it's way quicker to ask NumPy to
solve one system of equations

00:54:02.710 --> 00:54:04.810
than it it is to find the
inverse of a and then multiply

00:54:04.810 --> 00:54:06.490
it by b.

00:54:06.490 --> 00:54:07.350
And also,

00:54:07.350 --> 00:54:09.950
the act of finding the inverse

00:54:09.950 --> 00:54:13.590
introduces lots of numerical
precision issues that come from

00:54:13.590 --> 00:54:15.890
taking reciprocals of numbers.

00:54:15.890 --> 00:54:20.390
You saw that this formula, at
least in the 2 by 2 case,

00:54:21.590 --> 00:54:24.110
involved 1 over the determinant.

00:54:24.110 --> 00:54:26.430
But if the determinant is a
really small number, like the

00:54:26.430 --> 00:54:30.490
matrix is very close to having a
determinant of 0,

00:54:30.490 --> 00:54:32.330
then 1 over that is a really big
number.

00:54:32.330 --> 00:54:35.790
And if there's even a tiny bit
of floating point imprecision,

00:54:35.790 --> 00:54:38.630
this is sort of like some 370
stuff,

00:54:38.630 --> 00:54:41.630
but if there's even a tiny bit
of floating point imprecision in

00:54:41.630 --> 00:54:43.250
that really small determinant,

00:54:43.250 --> 00:54:46.530
it messes with the determinant
of the inverse of the matrix.

00:54:46.530 --> 00:54:50.090
And you can get results that are
less accurate than just solving

00:54:50.090 --> 00:54:51.590
the system directly,

00:54:51.590 --> 00:54:55.410
which is why in practice you
rarely,

00:54:55.410 --> 00:54:57.210
unless it's for teaching
purposes,

00:54:57.210 --> 00:54:59.490
ask for the inverse of a matrix.
You,

00:54:59.490 --> 00:55:03.230
instead, use np.lenalge.solve
to solve whatever system you

00:55:03.230 --> 00:55:04.730
actually need.

00:55:05.490 --> 00:55:06.210
Okay.

00:55:06.210 --> 00:55:08.210
So you should think of the
inverse and its existence as

00:55:08.210 --> 00:55:10.590
more of a conceptual idea that
we will need for whatever we're

00:55:10.590 --> 00:55:13.850
about to do, but not something
that you will calculate a ton

00:55:13.850 --> 00:55:14.850
directly.

