01:00:05.190 --> 01:00:09.450
Suppose A and B are two square
matrices.

01:00:09.450 --> 01:00:13.650
A is invertible and B isn't.
Is AB invertible?

01:00:13.650 --> 01:00:15.390
There are a few ways we can
think about this, but what do we

01:00:15.390 --> 01:00:16.390
think?

01:00:17.170 --> 01:00:19.330
Is AB invertible? Is it yes?

01:00:19.330 --> 01:00:21.250
Is it no? Is it maybe it
depends?

01:00:21.250 --> 01:00:21.990
Yeah.

01:00:21.990 --> 01:00:24.430
I mean, I just kind of thought
of it logically.

01:00:24.430 --> 01:00:28.250
Since B is not invertible, then
it's probably like linearly

01:00:28.250 --> 01:00:29.250
dependent?

01:00:29.450 --> 01:00:32.210
Its columns are linearly
dependent, yes.

01:00:32.210 --> 01:00:35.390
And then I feel like that would
just mean that you'd be shifting

01:00:35.390 --> 01:00:38.850
A all in one direction, which
would still make it linearly

01:00:38.850 --> 01:00:41.100
independent and invertible?

01:00:44.010 --> 01:00:46.170
So your perspective is,

01:00:46.170 --> 01:00:50.150
while the columns of B are
linearly dependent,

01:00:50.150 --> 01:00:53.190
the columns of A are linearly
independent.

01:00:53.190 --> 01:00:57.470
And even if we're taking these
linearly dependent linear

01:00:57.470 --> 01:00:59.790
combinations of a linearly
independent matrix,

01:00:59.790 --> 01:01:01.250
the result would still be
