01:01:01.250 --> 01:01:02.030
Yeah.

01:01:02.030 --> 01:01:04.370
OK, so that's one perspective.

01:01:04.370 --> 01:01:08.870
What other thoughts did we have?

01:01:13.470 --> 01:01:17.110
What other thoughts did we have?

01:01:17.110 --> 01:01:19.110
Well, here's another way to look
at it.

01:01:19.110 --> 01:01:21.270
If AB is invertible,

01:01:21.270 --> 01:01:24.270
then we need to be able to undo
the linear transformation of

01:01:24.270 --> 01:01:28.020
multiplying a vector by the
matrix AB.

01:01:33.890 --> 01:01:36.190
So if a,

01:01:36.410 --> 01:01:38.810
b is invertible,

01:01:41.850 --> 01:01:43.350
we must

01:01:44.230 --> 01:01:49.970
be able to undo this linear
transformation.

01:01:53.410 --> 01:01:58.110
What does that mean? It means
for any possible vector in

01:01:58.110 --> 01:02:01.330
Rn,

01:02:01.690 --> 01:02:06.750
I need to be able to tell you
the x that makes a b x equal to

01:02:06.750 --> 01:02:07.570
y.

01:02:07.570 --> 01:02:09.290
So for any possible y,

01:02:09.290 --> 01:02:14.610
you need to be able to solve for
x in y equals a b x.

01:02:14.610 --> 01:02:23.010
i.e.,

01:02:23.010 --> 01:02:38.090
OK,

01:02:39.630 --> 01:02:41.990
what this means is there should
be some formula that tells me

01:02:41.990 --> 01:02:44.010
what x is supposed to be.

01:02:44.010 --> 01:02:44.830
Now,

01:02:44.830 --> 01:02:47.080
A is invertible.

01:02:47.550 --> 01:02:52.800
So what does that mean I could
do here?

01:02:53.390 --> 01:02:54.670
Yeah.
Multiply by the inverse.

01:02:55.050 --> 01:02:56.950
Yeah, I can multiply both sides
by A inverse.

01:02:56.950 --> 01:03:01.030
That tells me A inverse Y is
equal to BX.

01:03:01.030 --> 01:03:01.610
But then,

01:03:01.610 --> 01:03:06.860
if we're trying to solve for X
here, what issue do we run into?

01:03:08.530 --> 01:03:11.890
B is not invertible, which means
at this stage, when we're trying

01:03:11.890 --> 01:03:14.730
to undo the transformation,

01:03:14.730 --> 01:03:19.370
there are multiple or maybe no
x's that make

01:03:19.370 --> 01:03:23.150
Bx equal to A inverse y.

01:03:23.150 --> 01:03:26.030
So this entire transformation
cannot be undone.

01:03:26.030 --> 01:03:30.230
While the second step of the
transformation can be undone,

01:03:30.230 --> 01:03:33.390
the first one can't because of
the premise that AB is not

01:03:33.390 --> 01:03:34.390
invertible.

01:03:36.070 --> 01:03:36.930
OK,

01:03:36.930 --> 01:03:38.330
here's another way to look at
it.

01:03:38.330 --> 01:03:40.710
Suppose B's action,

01:03:40.930 --> 01:03:43.410
pretend these matrices are two
by two.

01:03:43.410 --> 01:03:48.310
Suppose B's action is that it
deletes the y-coordinate of a

01:03:48.310 --> 01:03:49.130
vector,

01:03:49.130 --> 01:03:50.210
and it just

01:03:50.210 --> 01:03:52.250
projects it onto the x-axis.

01:03:52.250 --> 01:03:56.590
So it's like the matrix 1, 0, 0,
0.

01:03:56.590 --> 01:03:59.890
Then no matter what this A does,

01:03:59.890 --> 01:04:03.710
all of the results of A times Bx
are going to live on the same

01:04:03.710 --> 01:04:04.710
line,

01:04:06.130 --> 01:04:10.670
Because all of the BXs live on
the same line.

01:04:10.670 --> 01:04:13.190
These things all live on a line.

01:04:13.190 --> 01:04:15.270
So there's no way this can
spread those out if they all

01:04:15.270 --> 01:04:17.270
came from the same place to
begin with because this is a

01:04:17.270 --> 01:04:18.770
linear transformation.

01:04:19.970 --> 01:04:22.350
So we need every individual
matrix

01:04:22.350 --> 01:04:25.770
to be invertible if the product
is invertible.

01:04:25.770 --> 01:04:28.790
Okay,

01:04:29.790 --> 01:04:32.090
so here,

01:04:33.850 --> 01:04:38.010
but B is not invertible.

01:04:44.370 --> 01:04:48.120
So the answer is no.

01:04:52.170 --> 01:04:55.930
So a couple of thoughts here.

01:04:55.930 --> 01:05:03.670
If a and b are both n by n in an
invertible,

01:05:03.670 --> 01:05:07.930
what is the inverse of a times
b?

01:05:07.930 --> 01:05:11.590
We sort of implied it on the
previous page,

01:05:11.590 --> 01:05:12.890
but I've never actually stated
this.

01:05:12.890 --> 01:05:18.140
What is the inverse of A times
B?

01:05:23.810 --> 01:05:27.650
Is it A inverse times B inverse?

01:05:27.650 --> 01:05:29.850
Yeah.
B inverse times A inverse.

01:05:30.010 --> 01:05:31.310
Yeah, why?

01:05:31.310 --> 01:05:34.170
Because you're applying B first
and then A second.

01:05:34.170 --> 01:05:36.650
So to reverse it, you have to
reverse A first and then B

01:05:36.650 --> 01:05:37.650
second.
Exactly.

01:05:38.390 --> 01:05:40.550
Remember, think of this in terms
of applying linear

01:05:40.550 --> 01:05:43.550
transformations back to back.

01:05:43.710 --> 01:05:46.710
ABX does B first.

01:05:47.770 --> 01:05:50.510
It does B first and then A.

01:05:50.510 --> 01:05:53.010
So if you need to retrace your
steps and undo where you just

01:05:53.010 --> 01:05:53.750
went,

01:05:53.750 --> 01:05:56.170
you have to undo them in reverse
order.

01:05:56.170 --> 01:05:59.370
If you traveled from point A to
point B on Google Maps or

01:05:59.370 --> 01:06:02.150
whatever and need to go back to
where you came from,

01:06:02.150 --> 01:06:04.590
you retrace your steps in the
reverse order.

01:06:04.590 --> 01:06:06.390
This is the same idea.

01:06:06.390 --> 01:06:07.390
Similarly,

01:06:07.610 --> 01:06:10.630
another way to think about this
is if you have AB and multiply

01:06:10.630 --> 01:06:13.110
it by its inverse,

01:06:13.110 --> 01:06:16.170
well, then only when you swap
the order of the factors

01:06:16.170 --> 01:06:18.870
do things cancel out to the
identity, right? You need B to

01:06:18.870 --> 01:06:22.790
be next to its inverse in order
for those things to cancel out.

01:06:22.790 --> 01:06:24.710
Yeah, safe trip.

01:06:24.710 --> 01:06:27.350
And then you have A times A
inverse, and that gives you the

01:06:27.350 --> 01:06:28.350
identity as well.

01:06:28.350 --> 01:06:30.930
So the orders need to be
reversed,

01:06:30.930 --> 01:06:31.930
OK?

01:06:32.610 --> 01:06:35.610
Now, here's another related
idea.

01:06:38.370 --> 01:06:40.730
The determinant of AB,

01:06:40.730 --> 01:06:43.690
is that separable?

01:06:48.890 --> 01:06:50.330
Turns out it is,

01:06:50.330 --> 01:06:54.010
and you'll experiment with this
in lab more tomorrow.

01:06:54.010 --> 01:06:58.130
The determinant of a product is
equal to a product of

01:06:58.130 --> 01:06:59.130
determinants.

01:06:59.250 --> 01:07:01.770
And this is actually a really
easy way to see that if any one

01:07:01.770 --> 01:07:04.110
of the matrices is not
invertible,

01:07:04.110 --> 01:07:05.790
the whole thing can't be,
because then you'd have a

01:07:05.790 --> 01:07:08.550
determinant of 0 in here.

01:07:08.550 --> 01:07:10.770
Then the whole thing's
determinant would have to be 0.

01:07:10.770 --> 01:07:12.510
The way you should think about
this

01:07:12.510 --> 01:07:17.950
is that unit square is being
transformed after back-to-back

01:07:17.950 --> 01:07:19.450
linear transformations.

01:07:19.790 --> 01:07:23.310
Like if it gets rotated and then
stretched,

01:07:23.310 --> 01:07:27.890
the area of the new figure is
the result of performing each

01:07:27.890 --> 01:07:30.310
one of those transformations
separately and chaining them

01:07:30.310 --> 01:07:31.310
together.

01:07:32.990 --> 01:07:33.990
OK?

01:07:35.450 --> 01:07:38.730
You'll see this more in lab
tomorrow.

01:07:38.730 --> 01:07:40.810
One final idea.

01:07:40.810 --> 01:07:46.130
Suppose AB is invertible,

01:07:47.650 --> 01:07:53.510
but you know nothing else

01:07:53.510 --> 01:07:58.050
about A or B.

01:08:01.850 --> 01:08:03.350
R, A,

01:08:03.450 --> 01:08:05.700
and B, invertible.

01:08:08.330 --> 01:08:11.330
Discuss for a moment.

01:08:12.230 --> 01:08:15.980
And I'm emphasizing here, you
know nothing else.

01:08:16.130 --> 01:08:19.350
All you know is that AB is an
invertible matrix.

01:08:19.350 --> 01:08:21.990
R, A, and B, invertible.

01:08:21.990 --> 01:08:22.990
Non-invertible?

01:08:23.010 --> 01:08:24.610
Discuss.
Turn B 0 to the number of

01:08:24.610 --> 01:08:27.610
invertibles at the top?

01:08:28.270 --> 01:08:31.990
Is there a trick here with the
thing

01:08:31.990 --> 01:08:37.470
that might screw it up? Yeah,

01:08:38.150 --> 01:08:42.670
we might try to make an example
of that.

01:08:42.670 --> 01:08:47.170
But then they both put the
details in the product.

01:08:47.850 --> 01:08:50.150
Probably the best spot.

01:08:50.150 --> 01:08:53.850
Maybe this is a good case for
the art.

01:08:53.850 --> 01:08:56.010
I don't know,

01:08:56.010 --> 01:08:56.690
like,

01:08:56.690 --> 01:08:58.770
maybe they can't be interviewed
by each other.

01:08:58.770 --> 01:08:59.770
Really?

01:08:59.970 --> 01:09:02.150
Nah, I'm working on it.
But that would just give the

01:09:02.150 --> 01:09:04.230
matrices our schedule.

01:09:04.230 --> 01:09:07.170
I could break this.
I don't think you can create a

01:09:07.170 --> 01:09:08.170
person.

01:09:08.530 --> 01:09:10.030
We would still be inverted.

01:09:10.030 --> 01:09:10.950
Like, later,

01:09:10.950 --> 01:09:13.910
we'd just have to turn them into
the other two.

01:09:13.910 --> 01:09:14.630
Oh, OK.

01:09:14.630 --> 01:09:15.430
You need later.

01:09:15.430 --> 01:09:16.710
But it's a person.

01:09:16.710 --> 01:09:19.710
What are our thoughts?

01:09:19.870 --> 01:09:22.910
Suppose all you know is A times
B is a matrix that is

01:09:22.910 --> 01:09:23.910
invertible.

01:09:25.190 --> 01:09:26.670
That's all you're given,

01:09:26.670 --> 01:09:30.420
our A and B invertible.

01:09:30.670 --> 01:09:33.430
What did you think?

01:09:33.430 --> 01:09:36.970
Yeah? You said, like, there's a
chance that they would not be

01:09:36.970 --> 01:09:39.070
because they wouldn't have to be
squares.

01:09:39.070 --> 01:09:39.910
Exactly.

01:09:39.910 --> 01:09:43.550
The key is that they don't have
to be square matrices.

01:09:43.550 --> 01:09:47.270
It is perfectly possible to
multiply two non-square matrices

01:09:47.270 --> 01:09:51.020
and get an invertible square
matrix.

01:09:51.990 --> 01:09:54.390
Now, if they are square,

01:09:54.390 --> 01:09:57.010
then the answer is yes, they
have to be because of the logic

01:09:57.010 --> 01:09:58.430
we just discussed.

01:09:58.430 --> 01:10:00.690
The determinant of their product
has to be the product of the

01:10:00.690 --> 01:10:03.350
individual determinants.
And if this is not 0,

01:10:04.990 --> 01:10:09.490
well, then these can't be 0
either.

01:10:12.530 --> 01:10:17.030
So the answer is not necessarily

01:10:18.390 --> 01:10:24.390
because A and B don't have to be
square.

01:10:31.190 --> 01:10:33.630
If they are square,

01:10:33.630 --> 01:10:36.630
they must be invertible.

01:10:40.570 --> 01:10:43.450
Now, can you give me an easy
example of an a and b that are

01:10:43.450 --> 01:10:48.700
not square, but a times b is
invertible?

01:10:50.190 --> 01:10:53.210
There should be like a default
example that comes to mind based

01:10:53.210 --> 01:10:56.210
on recent homework examples.

01:11:01.050 --> 01:11:02.550
Any ideas?

01:11:09.370 --> 01:11:10.570
So remember,

01:11:10.570 --> 01:11:13.670
a few times now I've mentioned
that no matter what the shape of

01:11:13.670 --> 01:11:15.920
a matrix is,

01:11:16.070 --> 01:11:18.630
it has a friend or has two
friends who are both square.

01:11:18.630 --> 01:11:21.630
What am I getting?

01:11:22.230 --> 01:11:25.930
Pretend x is a 5 by 2 matrix,

01:11:25.930 --> 01:11:27.430
not square.

01:11:27.490 --> 01:11:31.970
There is a matrix that I can
make with x that is square.

01:11:31.970 --> 01:11:34.550
There are two, in fact.

01:11:34.550 --> 01:11:35.550
Yeah?

01:11:35.750 --> 01:11:37.630
A times A transpose.

01:11:37.630 --> 01:11:41.410
Yeah, I can multiply it by its
transpose in either order.

01:11:41.410 --> 01:11:42.410
Remember,

01:11:47.010 --> 01:11:51.050
if A is n by d,

01:11:51.050 --> 01:11:55.670
then A transpose A is d by d.

01:11:55.670 --> 01:11:58.870
And A A transpose is n by n.

01:11:58.870 --> 01:12:03.030
So if I just find a matrix A,

01:12:03.030 --> 01:12:06.330
or let me just say, I can find a
matrix A

01:12:06.330 --> 01:12:12.230
and maybe put the right numbers
in it so that A transpose

01:12:12.230 --> 01:12:15.590
A is equal to the identity
matrix.

01:12:15.590 --> 01:12:18.650
Or I didn't have to say identity
matrix, an invertible matrix,

01:12:18.650 --> 01:12:21.410
and then this would be possible.
I'd just say B is equal to A

01:12:21.410 --> 01:12:22.410
transpose.

01:12:24.490 --> 01:12:25.310
OK?

01:12:25.310 --> 01:12:25.930
Now,

01:12:25.930 --> 01:12:27.710
what was the interpretation

01:12:27.710 --> 01:12:32.960
of the things in A transpose A?
This has come up a lot.

01:12:38.930 --> 01:12:42.610
A transpose A or X transpose X.

01:12:42.610 --> 01:12:45.530
We're going to start using this
when we talk about regression as

01:12:45.530 --> 01:12:46.270
well.

01:12:46.270 --> 01:12:49.230
What is contained in that
matrix?

01:12:49.230 --> 01:12:52.070
There's an easy English
interpretation of the entries of

01:12:52.070 --> 01:12:54.320
A transpose A.

01:12:54.370 --> 01:12:56.620
What was that?

01:13:09.270 --> 01:13:11.550
Remember, I'll give you a hint.
What's the shape of A transpose

01:13:11.550 --> 01:13:13.110
A? It's d by d.

01:13:13.110 --> 01:13:15.730
And what is d with regards to A?

01:13:15.730 --> 01:13:18.050
It's its number of columns.

01:13:18.050 --> 01:13:20.290
Something about the columns.

01:13:20.290 --> 01:13:22.310
It's like the dot product of the
columns of the columns.

01:13:22.310 --> 01:13:26.810
Yeah, it's the dot products of
all pairs of columns.

01:13:35.530 --> 01:13:40.250
So if I tell you that for some
matrix,

01:13:40.250 --> 01:13:43.530
I think we did this as an
example a few lectures ago,

01:13:43.530 --> 01:13:47.270
A transpose A is 1, 0, 0, 1,
what does this tell you?

01:13:47.270 --> 01:13:50.650
It tells you column 1 is a unit
vector,

01:13:50.650 --> 01:13:52.870
because its dot product with
itself is 1.

01:13:52.870 --> 01:13:55.710
So its squared length is 1, so
its length is 1.

01:13:55.710 --> 01:13:57.710
Column 2 is a unit vector.

01:13:57.710 --> 01:13:59.790
And column 1 and column 2 are
orthogonal.

01:13:59.790 --> 01:14:04.410
But I could make that set up for

01:14:04.410 --> 01:14:07.350
vectors of any shape,

01:14:07.350 --> 01:14:08.350
really.

01:14:09.130 --> 01:14:11.380
So for example,

01:14:13.090 --> 01:14:18.230
let's make a clearly not square
example, like

01:14:18.230 --> 01:14:22.930
3 fifths,

01:14:23.290 --> 01:14:23.790
0,

01:14:23.790 --> 01:14:24.490
0,

01:14:24.490 --> 01:14:26.740
4 fifths, 0,

01:14:27.010 --> 01:14:29.990
and 4 fifths, 0,

01:14:29.990 --> 01:14:30.990
0,

01:14:31.130 --> 01:14:34.130
negative 3 fifths, 0.

01:14:34.870 --> 01:14:38.070
These columns are both unit
vectors,

01:14:38.070 --> 01:14:41.310
because remember 3 squared plus
4 squared equals 5 squared.

01:14:41.310 --> 01:14:44.930
So if I divide those both by 5,
I get 3 fifths squared plus 4

01:14:44.930 --> 01:14:47.810
fifths squared is equal to 1.
These zeros don't really change

01:14:47.810 --> 01:14:48.810
anything.

01:14:48.890 --> 01:14:51.230
And if I take the dot product of
these two columns with each

01:14:51.230 --> 01:14:52.530
other, I get 0.

01:14:52.530 --> 01:14:55.250
So this is a matrix where A
transpose A is equal to the

01:14:55.250 --> 01:14:58.250
identity matrix, which is
invertible.

01:14:58.750 --> 01:15:03.250
But the matrix is not square.

01:15:07.790 --> 01:15:10.850
So is it possible to multiply
two matrices and get an

01:15:10.850 --> 01:15:13.190
invertible matrix, but the two
things you multiplied are not

01:15:13.190 --> 01:15:13.990
invertible?

01:15:13.990 --> 01:15:15.970
Yes, because the things you
multiply don't have to be

01:15:15.970 --> 01:15:16.970
square.

01:15:17.010 --> 01:15:20.760
And only square matrices could
be invertible.

